{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_113b45178c7f",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5463,
      "mechanism": "The divider reload branch in the slow-clock counter (llsdspi) currently triggers on every startup_hold or byte_accepted event. The observed failure shows o_sck stuck high from cycle 101 onward, meaning the SPI bit-clock never starts toggling. Adding && (r_z_counter) ensures the reload only occurs when the previous divider period has fully elapsed (r_z_counter==1), preventing premature counter reset that would hold the clock high indefinitely. This matches the cycle-101 mismatch on o_sck."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_d0284aacab57",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5284,
      "mechanism": "The same divider reload logic also has a branch gated by !startup_hold && (!i_cs || (OPT_SPI_ARBITRATION && !i_bus_grant)). The failure shows the first 101 cycles pass but o_sck goes high and stays high at cycle 101. Qualifying this branch with && (o_sclk) makes the counter reset sensitive to the current output clock phase, ensuring the divider only reloads on a clean clock edge rather than asynchronously. This directly addresses the first mismatched cycle on o_sck."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_6d5da8d9d65c",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5284,
      "mechanism": "Alternative to the previous patch: qualifying the same branch with && (r_z_counter) instead of o_sclk. Since r_z_counter asserts exactly when the divider hits terminal count, this also prevents early reloads that would freeze o_sck high. The failure trace first diverges at cycle 101 on o_sck, consistent with a divider that never reaches terminal count because it keeps being reset."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_8ef695416481",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5463,
      "mechanism": "The startup/byte_accepted reload branch is also a candidate for the opposite polarity guard: && (!r_z_counter). If the design intent is to reload only while the counter is actively counting down (not at terminal count), this would prevent a stuck-high divider. The first mismatch on o_sck at cycle 101 is consistent with either polarity error; this patch tests the alternate polarity."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_baa9767c5bfd",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4843,
      "mechanism": "In the WAIT_FOR_STARTUP generate block, the startup_counter decrement is gated by |startup_counter. The failure shows divergence starting at cycle 101, exactly after the 75-cycle startup window (STARTUP_CLOCKS=75, first sample at cycle 96). Qualifying the |startup_counter condition with && (!i_cs) ensures the startup timer only runs while chip-select is asserted, matching the SPI protocol where startup clocks are issued only after CS goes low. The observed o_sck freeze at cycle 101 aligns with a startup counter that expired incorrectly."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_8b77b8c988e9",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4431,
      "mechanism": "The generate block’s startup_hold initialization uses STARTUP_CLOCKS > 0 as a compile-time constant. The simulation uses explicit initial values, so the block is active. Adding && (startup_hold) to the condition makes the startup-clock-toggle logic (o_sclk <= !o_sclk when r_z_counter) explicitly dependent on the runtime startup_hold flag, preventing spurious toggles before the counter loads. The first o_sck mismatch at cycle 101 occurs right after the startup window, suggesting the startup toggle sequence malfunctioned."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_04bb6f387ef0",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4790,
      "mechanism": "Also in the startup generate block: the condition startup_hold && !past_sclk && o_sclk controls the startup counter decrement. Qualifying with && (!i_bus_grant) ties the startup countdown to the bus-grant arbitration signal. Since the testbench drives i_bus_grant in the failing trace, a missing grant could stall the startup counter, leaving o_sck frozen high from cycle 101 onward. The trace’s first input window shows i_bus_grant=1 at cycle 96, so this path is active."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_4cf862bc9372",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4843,
      "mechanism": "Same startup_counter decrement line as patch_baa9767c5bfd but qualifying with && (i_cs) instead of !i_cs. If the startup timer should only count when CS is asserted (active-low in the top module but inverted in llsdspi), this polarity would be correct. The cycle-101 o_sck mismatch is the observable symptom of whichever polarity is wrong."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "All eight patches target the two clock-generation sites that can freeze o_sck high: the slow-clock divider in llsdspi (lines 5284 and 5463) and the startup-clock generator in the WAIT_FOR_STARTUP block (lines 4431, 4790, 4843). The public failure shows o_sck stuck at 1 from cycle 101 onward, which is exactly the symptom of a divider that never reaches terminal count or a startup sequence that never releases the clock. Each patch adds a qualifying condition drawn from nearby state variables (r_z_counter, o_sclk, startup_hold, i_cs, i_bus_grant) to prevent premature or spurious counter reloads. Ranking favors the divider reload branches first because they gate the ongoing SPI bit clock, then the startup block because the first mismatch occurs immediately after the 75-cycle startup window."
}
